 ## OTA Update Mechanism
 
-The factory firmware fetches updates from `http://murphy.pandacat.ai/ota/latest`.
-The endpoint returns a JSON manifest:
+The factory firmware fetches updates from `https://murphy.pandacat.ai/ota/latest`.
+The endpoint returns a JSON manifest carrying the whole install recipe:
 ```json
-{ "firmware_url": "...", "firmware_size": ... }
+{ "version": "2.3.5",
+  "firmware_url": "https://murphy.pandacat.ai/firmware/panda-ai-os-2.3.5.bin",
+  "firmware_size": 4319904, "app_offset": 131072,
+  "product": "murphy-os", "board": "mofei",
+  "sha256": "d049bfc8e5d44c8f0be6c8f69855a9b36954e3df053ffecaa5b86dfe449dfebd" }
 ```
 The device then downloads the binary and writes it to the `app1` OTA slot.
 This mechanism can be intercepted by running a fake OTA server on a local
 network — useful as an alternative to USB flashing.
+
+`board` is `"mofei"` with no model discriminator, and the response does not vary
+with query parameters — one endpoint serves the whole product line. Superseded
+builds stay reachable at their original `/firmware/panda-ai-os-<version>.bin`
+URL, but no index of them is published. The current line is **Panda AI OS**, not
+the `murphy-26-0526-1.2.16.bin` "Murphy Reader" image analysed elsewhere here;
+it logs its complete hardware configuration at boot, which is analysed in
+[panda_ai_os.md](panda_ai_os.md).
